# =============================================================================
# NEMO 87 - RELAY: more download sources, one verified chain, a Drive link you can open
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YouTube downloads kept failing although a download system existed. What the code review of v76-v78 found:
#  1. ONE source. Every "download <link>" goes through the v77/v78 manager, which only ever ran yt-dlp. The independent engines built in
#     v59-v62 (pytubefix, YouTube.js, Piped/Invidious mirrors, Cobalt) were no longer reached.
#  2. yt-dlp was never refreshed and only one client profile was tried; a Node.js runtime was not used when Deno was missing; the
#     solver (EJS) had no fallback; your /proxy was ignored; a missing "bv*[height<=N]" format ended the job instead of falling back.
#  3. Failures were reported as "Transfer or verification failed" - no source, no reason, no next step.
#  4. Drive links were private by design ("restricted link"), so a link you could not open was reported as success.
# v87 keeps the v76-v78 queue, size/disk guards, Drive upload and verification, and changes how a video is obtained and shared:
#  * a SOURCE CHAIN, each source verified (ffprobe) and time-boxed: yt-dlp (6 client profiles, node/deno runtime, cookies, proxy, one
#    automatic yt-dlp upgrade on extractor errors) -> pytubefix -> YouTube.js -> Piped mirrors -> Invidious mirrors -> Cobalt (if configured);
#  * the source that worked last is tried first next time; every failure is classified and reported per source with the next best step;
#  * Drive links are shared as "anyone with the link can view" (or with one e-mail, or kept private) - your choice, by chat;
#  * natural-language controls: "download sources", "test youtube download", "set up download sources", "share my last download".
# Credentials, owner lock, trading guards, permissions, backup and rollback are untouched.
# =============================================================================
